        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "5eeaa3be-0a71-592f-a9f6-892e3ede8787",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Products: 5.6×61mm SE, 5.6×61mm VHSE, 5.6×61mm Vom Hofe Super Express, 6.5×55mm\n- Places: Germany, Sweden, Norway, United Kingdoms of Sweden and Norway\n- Organizations: governments of Sweden and Norway\n- Products: Krag–Jørgensen rifle, Mauser rifle\n\nFacts:\n- The 6.5×55mm cartridge was developed in 1891 for new service rifles under consideration by the United Kingdoms of Sweden and Norway.\n- Sweden and Norway had independent armies, so their governments followed the practice of using the same ammunition while purchasing different small arms.\n- Norway adopted the Krag–Jørgensen rifle, while Sweden adopted a Mauser rifle design.\n- The 5.6×61mm SE is an abbreviated name for the 5.6×61mm Vom Hofe Super Express rifle cartridge.\n- The 5.6×61mm Vom Hofe Super Express rifle cartridge was introduced in Germany in 1937.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "9f327172-d830-5d53-a0c5-dda5fa88ff10",
        "text": "Page: 5.6×61mm VHSE. Sentence: The 5.6×61mm SE, an abbreviated form of its full designation, the 5.6×61mm [Vom Hofe] Super Express, is a rifle cartridge introduced in Germany in 1937.\nPage: 6.5×55mm. Sentence: It was developed in 1891 for use in the new service rifles then under consideration by the United Kingdoms of Sweden and Norway.\nPage: 6.5×55mm. Sentence: The two nations had independent armies and consequently the normal procedure at the time was for their respective governments to use the same ammunition and then purchase small arms of their choice.\nPage: 6.5×55mm. Sentence: Norway adopted the Krag–Jørgensen rifle, while Sweden adopted a Mauser rifle design.",
        "type": "DocumentChunk"
